<Layout title="About">
  <p class="section-label">About</p>
  <h1>Why I do this work</h1>

  <p>
    I'm fascinated by how intelligence emerges from the interaction between memory, perception, and decision-making. 
    We can watch a brain adapt to its environment, but we still understand remarkably little about the internal computations that transform what we see into what we remember and ultimately how we act. 
  </p>

  <h2>Background</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>University of Chicago — Molecular Engineering (Quantum Track) and Computational Neuroscience</li>
    <li>Marlene Cohen Lab</li>
    <li>Sihong Wang Research Group</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Long-term goals</h2>
  <p>
    I plan to pursue a PhD at the intersection of computational neuroscience and machine learning, focusing on how internal representations give rise to adaptive behavior. 
    I'm particularly interested in developing methods that recover computation from high-dimensional neural activity and comparing those representations across biological and artificial systems. 
    I hope that my background in molecular and quantum engineering complements this work by grounding me in systems thinking and rigorous engineering design, shaping how I approach scientific questions as much as the questions themselves.
     Ultimately, I hope to build computational foundations that make future neurotechnologies and adaptive AI systems more interpretable, reliable, and scientifically grounded.
  </p>
